    question: 'Can I use a quitclaim deed to transfer property to my minor child?',
    answer:
      'Not without complications. Florida minors can hold title to real property, but because they lack legal capacity to independently execute deeds or contracts, direct ownership by a minor can create substantial management, sale and financing complications. A later sale, mortgage or other transaction may require guardianship, court approval or another legally authorized representative, depending on the circumstances. Consult a Florida attorney before putting real estate into a minor/custodial arrangement.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the difference between a quitclaim deed and a warranty deed for family transfers?',
    answer:
      'A quitclaim deed transfers whatever the grantor owns with no guarantee about title quality. A warranty deed guarantees clear title and obligates the grantor to defend against title claims. For trusted family transfers with clean title, a quitclaim deed is generally sufficient. If the recipient needs title assurance — for example, to immediately refinance or sell — a warranty deed or special warranty deed is safer.',
