  <h1 class="page-h1">Florida Quitclaim Deed Between Family Members: What to Know Before You Sign</h1>

  <p class="last-updated">Updated August 7, 2026 using the primary authorities cited below. Not legal advice.</p>

  <!-- Intro -->
  <p>
    Transferring property to a parent, child, or sibling in Florida is one of the most common reasons
    people reach for a quitclaim deed. The process is simpler than a traditional sale — no title search,
    no title insurance, no closing agent required — because you are dealing with someone you trust completely.
  </p>
  <p>
    But "simple" does not mean "consequence-free." A Florida family quitclaim deed touches federal gift
    tax rules, state documentary stamp obligations, homestead exemption portability, and — for owners
    who may later seek Medicaid long-term-care benefits — a five-year Medicaid lookback that most deed
    pages never mention.
  </p>

  <!-- DeedForm CTA -->
