    <h2 class="cta-block__heading">Generate Your Free Florida Quitclaim Deed</h2>
    <p class="cta-block__body">
      Florida-specific form with two-witness blocks, notary acknowledgment, and the correct legal
      description field. Instant download.
    </p>
    <DeedForm />
  </div>

  <Disclaimer />

  <!-- Why Families Use Quitclaim Deeds -->
  <h2>Why Families Use Quitclaim Deeds</h2>
  <p>
    A <a href="/quitclaim-deed/florida/">quitclaim deed</a> conveys whatever interest the grantor currently
    holds — no warranties, no promises about title history. That trade-off is perfectly sensible inside
    a family because:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>You already know the property's history.</li>
    <li>No sale is involved. There is no purchase price, no lender, no escrow.</li>
    <li>Estate planning is the goal — moving property out of a parent's estate or into a trust is often cleaner with a quitclaim deed than waiting for probate.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Common family transfer scenarios: parent-to-child transfers, adding a spouse to title, removing a
    deceased spouse from title, transferring to a revocable living trust, and sibling-to-sibling division
    of inherited property.
  </p>

  <!-- When Appropriate -->
  <h2>When a Quitclaim Deed Is Appropriate — and When It Is Not</h2>
  <p><strong>Appropriate for family transfers when:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Title is clean: the grantor owns the property outright or with a mortgage, with no unresolved liens, judgments, or title disputes</li>
    <li>The transfer is a gift or a nominal-consideration estate-planning move between trusted parties</li>
    <li>Both parties understand that the grantor conveys only what they own</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Not appropriate when:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>The property has title defects — unresolved liens, boundary disputes, or clouded ownership — because a quitclaim deed passes those problems directly to the recipient with no legal recourse</li>
    <li>The recipient needs assurance of clean title (for example, a child who plans to sell or refinance soon after receiving it) — a general warranty deed or special warranty deed is safer in those cases</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Gift Tax -->
  <h2>Federal Gift Tax: The Most Misunderstood Issue</h2>
  <p>
    When you transfer property to a family member for less than its fair market value — including a
    transfer for $0 — the IRS treats the difference as a gift. Neither of the two reasons this matters
    is "you immediately owe tax."
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The annual gift exclusion.</strong> For 2026, a donor generally can give up to <strong>$19,000</strong>
    of qualifying present-interest gifts to each recipient under the annual gift-tax exclusion without
    the gift itself requiring a federal gift-tax return solely because of its amount. Verify the current
    figure at IRS.gov.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The lifetime exemption.</strong> The 2026 federal basic exclusion is <strong>$15 million per
    person</strong>. These amounts are subject to change — verify current figures at IRS.gov. A
    real-estate gift may require the donor to file Form 709, but filing does not necessarily mean tax
    is payable; it may only reduce the remaining lifetime exclusion.
  </p>
  <p>
    Florida has no separate state gift tax. Federal gift-tax reporting is generally the donor's responsibility,
    not the recipient's.
  </p>

  <!-- Documentary Stamp Tax -->
  <h2>Florida Documentary Stamp Tax on Family Transfers</h2>
  <p>
    Florida imposes a documentary stamp tax on most deed transfers under <strong>Fla. Stat. § 201.02</strong>.
    The rate is $0.70 per $100 (or fraction thereof) of consideration.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Transfer with no consideration and no mortgage:</strong> If a parent gifts a property to a
      child outright with genuinely no consideration — no stated nominal consideration, no relevant
      debt, lien or encumbrance — documentary stamp tax generally does not apply.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Transfer with an outstanding mortgage:</strong> An outstanding mortgage, lien or other
      encumbrance can constitute consideration for documentary-stamp purposes even when the transfer is
      intended as a gift. Florida treats the outstanding mortgage balance as "consideration" for stamp
      tax purposes — whether or not the grantee formally assumes the debt.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Florida DOR Rule 12B-4.013 addresses family transfer exemptions in more detail. If you are uncertain
    whether your specific transfer triggers documentary stamps, confirm with a Florida attorney before recording.
  </p>

  <!-- Homestead Exemption -->
  <h2>Florida Homestead Exemption: It Does Not Transfer Automatically</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>$50,000 homestead exemption</strong> reduces the assessed value of a Florida homestead
    by up to $50,000 for property tax purposes. When ownership changes, the new owner must apply
    separately with the county property appraiser's office — typically by <strong>March 1</strong> of
    the first year they own and occupy the property as their primary residence. Missing the deadline
    means losing that year's exemption entirely.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Save Our Homes (SOH) cap:</strong> Florida's Save Our Homes assessment cap limits the annual
    increase in assessed value for homestead properties to 3% or the rate of inflation, whichever is
    lower. When ownership transfers, the new owner starts fresh — the assessed value resets to market
    value at the time of transfer.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>SOH portability:</strong> Florida allows a homestead owner to "port" a built-up SOH differential
    to a new homestead property. If the grantor is moving to a new primary residence, they can port
    their accumulated SOH savings by filing the portability application within 3 years of the transfer.
    Portability rules are worth a conversation with the county property appraiser's office.
  </p>

  <!-- Medicaid Lookback -->
  <h2>Medicaid Lookback: The Issue Most Deed Pages Ignore</h2>
  <Callout type="warning" title="Critical: Medicaid five-year lookback applies to family property transfers">
    <p>
      Florida Medicaid uses a <strong>five-year lookback period</strong> when evaluating eligibility for
      long-term care benefits (nursing home coverage). If an applicant transferred assets — including
      real property — for less than fair market value within five years before applying for Medicaid,
      the state calculates a penalty period during which Medicaid will not pay for care, regardless of
      the applicant's financial need at the time of application.
    </p>
    <p>
      Gifting a home outright to a child is exactly the kind of transfer that triggers this review.
      <strong>This applies even when the transfer is clearly motivated by estate planning.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>If the owner may seek Medicaid long-term-care benefits within the applicable lookback
      period, obtain Florida elder-law advice before transferring the property. Consult a Florida elder
      law attorney before signing anything.</strong> Legitimate Medicaid-planning strategies exist —
      including certain trust structures, life estate arrangements, and carefully timed transfers — but
      they require individualized legal advice, not a template deed.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <!-- Minor Children -->
  <h2>Transferring Property to a Minor Child</h2>
  <p>
    Florida minors can hold title to real property, but because they lack legal capacity to independently
    execute deeds or contracts, direct ownership by a minor can create substantial management, sale and
    financing complications. A later sale, mortgage or other transaction may require guardianship, court
    approval or another legally authorized representative. If the intended recipient is under 18, potential
    structures include:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Florida Uniform Transfers to Minors Act (FUTMA) custodianship</strong> — requires careful
      deed drafting; consult a Florida attorney to confirm whether FUTMA is appropriate for real estate
      in the specific circumstances.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Hold in trust</strong> — a trust can hold the property for the child's benefit with a
      trustee managing it under the trust's terms, offering more flexibility and control over when
      and how the child gains full ownership.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wait until the child turns 18</strong> — for transfers that are not time-sensitive,
      deferring until majority is the most straightforward path.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <!-- Execution Requirements -->
  <h2>Florida Deed Execution Requirements</h2>
  <p>
    Florida Statute §689.01 generally requires a deed conveying real estate to be signed by the grantor
    in the presence of two subscribing witnesses. To record the deed, its execution generally must also
    be acknowledged or proved as provided in Fla. Stat. §695.03.
  </p>
  <p>In practice, a Florida deed requires:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Two subscribing witnesses</strong> — both must be present when the grantor signs and must themselves sign the deed</li>
    <li><strong>Acknowledgment for recording</strong> — a notary public executes a formal acknowledgment block as required by §695.03</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    After execution, the deed must be recorded with the <strong>Clerk of the Circuit Court</strong> in
    the county where the property is located.
  </p>

  <!-- Step-by-Step -->
  <h2>Step-by-Step: Florida Family Property Transfer</h2>
  <div class="process-steps">
    <ProcessStep
      number={1}
      title="Prepare the deed"
